# Explain one topic across a mini-hub

**Reference pattern.** These examples are simulated. They do not install Home Assistant screens or control a device.

A mini-hub is a guide, not a setup wizard. People read the part they need, in any order, without submitting answers or changing a device.

## Use the contents list

The [contents list guide](../components/contents-list.html#choose-the-navigation-scope) owns the navigation. This pattern puts it on a small group of real pages, with the same contents and Previous and Next links on each. Keep it separate from an in-page list and from a question flow that saves answers.

Use two to eight pages, for a topic such as understanding an energy chart.

## Make the current page clear

Lead with the subject of the current page and put the wider topic below it as a caption. A reader who follows a link must see at once that the page changed. If the heading holds both parts, add a visually hidden separator for screen readers.

<%= example('nhs-mini-hub-reading') %>

## Offer direct and sequential navigation

Keep the contents in the same order on every page. Mark the current item with `aria-current="page"`. At the end, add Previous and Next links that use the real page titles (WG-083). These are ordinary links, not buttons that save data. The two examples are separate pages that link to each other.

<%= example('nhs-mini-hub-gaps') %>

## Choose the right scope

“Your energy chart” can cover reading units, missing data and comparing periods. It must not also cover account settings or vacuum controls. If the content fits on one page, use headings and optional in-page links. If there are many different topics, use a [hub](hub.html). If each step asks for information, use [question pages](question-pages.html).

## Accessibility and content

Keep headings, link names and browser titles specific to the current page. Keep a readable line length and a single column at narrow width. Use ordinary links so Back, bookmarks and opening in a new tab work. Do not make people finish earlier pages before they can read a later one.

Mark the current contents item with text or another visible cue, not colour alone (WG-063). Keep the repeated navigation short. Test the guide with people who use magnification or heading navigation in a screen reader.

## Home Assistant boundary

A mini-hub can explain native chart meanings, availability states or household maintenance records. It must not pose as the native Energy panel or show invented values as real readings. Link to a device or chart only where the installation supports that route. Reading a guide must never run an action, turn on an automation or clear an alert.

## Keep it current

When a capability changes, review the group as one guide. Keep the contents and the Previous and Next links the same as the published set. Keep old links that still mean something, and remove references to retired controls. The preview uses invented explanatory text and the same links in the embedded and standalone views.
